# Words: setting, getting, quoting, unset and protected.
# Origin: REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3, section 5.

--- id words/set-word-assigns-and-yields
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.3
--- note A set-word produces the value it assigned, which is why chained
--- note assignment works at all.
--- code
age: 42
--- result
42

--- id words/chained-assignment
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.3
--- code
a: b: 10
add a b
--- result
20

--- id words/word-lookup
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5
--- code
zero: 0
zero
--- result
0

--- id words/literal-word-strips-one-quote
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.5
--- note 'this evaluates to the word this, and that word is not then
--- note looked up. Without this, code could not be data.
--- code
word: 'this
word
--- result
this

--- id words/literal-word-of-a-native-name
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.5
--- note Even a word that names a function comes back as a word, not as
--- note the function and not by calling it.
--- code
word: 'print
word
--- result
print

--- id words/get-word-fetches-without-calling
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.4
--- requires control
--- note :print gives the function itself. This is how a function gets
--- note passed to another function.
--- code
native? :if
--- result
#(true)

--- id words/get-word-aliases-a-function
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.4
--- requires output
--- code
drucken: :print
drucken "test"
--- prints
test

--- id words/get-word-via-get
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.4
--- requires output
--- code
stampa: get 'print
stampa "test"
--- prints
test

--- id words/first-of-block-gives-a-word
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.5
--- requires series
--- note Taking a value out of a block does not evaluate it, so the word
--- note arrives as a word.
--- code
word: first [this and that]
word
--- result
this

--- id words/unset-word-raises
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.6
--- code
outlook
--- error script no-value

--- id words/value-of-unset-word
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.6
--- code
word: "here"
unset 'word
value? 'word
--- result
#(false)

--- id words/value-of-set-word
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.6
--- code
word: "here"
value? 'word
--- result
#(true)

--- id words/protected-word-refuses-assignment
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.7
--- code
word: "here"
protect 'word
word: "there"
--- error script locked-word

--- id words/unprotect-restores-assignment
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.7
--- code
word: "here"
protect 'word
unprotect 'word
word: "there"
--- result
"there"

--- id words/set-word-counts-as-one-value
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.1
--- requires series
--- note age: 42 is two values because the colon is part of the word.
--- code
length? [age: 42]
--- result
2

--- id words/equals-is-a-separate-word
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.1
--- requires series
--- note age = 42 is three values, because = is its own word. This is the
--- note clearest demonstration that a set-word is lexically one thing.
--- code
length? [age = 42]
--- result
3

--- id words/set-multiple-words
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.3
--- requires series
--- code
set [number num ten] 10
add number add num ten
--- result
30

--- id words/set-words-from-a-block
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.3
--- requires series
--- code
set [one two three] [1 2 3]
three
--- result
3

--- id words/select-by-literal-word
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.5
--- requires series
--- note A block of data holding words, selected by a quoted word. The
--- note block is never evaluated, which is the whole point.
--- code
video: [
    title "Independence Day"
    length 2:25:24
    date   4/july/1996
]
select video 'title
--- result
"Independence Day"

--- id words/set-word-at-block-end-raises
--- origin JEBOL spec/eval.allium, rule SetWordEvaluates
--- note Not in the guide. Specified as an error rather than an
--- note assignment of UNSET, because `x:` alone is a mistake rather than
--- note an instruction to clear x. Listed here so the decision is tested.
--- code
do [age:]
--- error script need-value

--- id words/a-number-splits-from-a-trailing-angle-bracket
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note A number followed by a run of symbols is two values, not one.
--- note The bracket is not part of the number and does not begin a tag.
--- code
mold load "19-Jan-2010<"
--- result
"[19-Jan-2010 <]"

--- id words/a-run-of-symbols-is-a-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Angle brackets cannot appear in a word spelled with letters, yet
--- note a lexeme made only of symbols is a word whatever it contains.
--- note That is how <, <= and --> are all legal names.
--- code
mold reduce [type? first [<] type? first [<=] type? first [-->]]
--- result
"[#(word!) #(word!) #(word!)]"

--- id words/letters-mixed-with-an-angle-bracket-are-refused
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Not the word a followed by the word <. The number case splits and
--- note the letter case refuses, so the decision needs the lexeme's type.
--- code
load "a<"
--- error syntax invalid
